#. §0: `linear₂`
#. §1: `D N → D`
#: Game.Levels.Demo.L02_Overgeneration
msgid "The proof went through, and that is the problem. *a a the this dog* is not an expression of\n"
"English, yet §0 derives it, so the rule is too strong. Rule 4 takes an N and returns an N,\n"
"so it can apply again and again.\n"
"\n"
"Suppose instead we keep only Rule 3, §1, and set the goal to D. Then *this dog* becomes a D,\n"
"no rule applies after that, and ⟨a a the this dog, D⟩ cannot be derived. That is the result we want.\n"
"Failing to derive is also a good result: a good model should not allow more than the natural\n"
"language does."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `rule \"NAME\" x y …`
#. §1: `the_cat_DP`
#. §2: `\"X0 YP -> X'\"`
#. §3: `\"X⁰ YP → X′\"`
#. §4: `as NAME`
#: Game.Metadata
msgid "§0 applies the rule NAME to the expressions x, y, … in\n"
"your hypotheses. The rule computes the result, which appears as a new\n"
"hypothesis with a readable name (for example §1). If the rule is not\n"
"defined on those inputs, it is rejected.\n"
"\n"
"Rule names may be typed with ASCII: §2 means §3.\n"
"Add §4 at the end to choose the name of the result yourself."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.Demo.L04_MP
msgid "Look at the last step. Before Internal MERGE, {DP, vP} had no label: neither D nor v\n"
"shares a feature with the other. After it, the lower DP is a copy, the labeling algorithm\n"
"sees only vP, and the new root {DP, TP} is labeled ⟨φ,φ⟩ by shared φ.\n"
"Externalization pronounced only the higher copy: *the cat will sleep*.\n"
"\n"
"Three levels, three generations of generative syntax, one language.\n"
"In each, a grammar is categories, a lexicon and rules — data — and Lean checks every step."
msgstr ""

#: Game
msgid "This demo shows one formal language expressing grammars from three eras of generative syntax:\n"
"\n"
"1. a linear grammar,\n"
"2. X-bar theory with GB modules (c-selection, Case, EPP, Infl lowering),\n"
"3. the Minimalist Program of the workspace era (MERGE on workspaces, labeling).\n"
"\n"
"The language is Bare Grammar (Keenan & Stabler 2003), implemented in Lean 4. A grammar is\n"
"data — categories, a lexicon, rules — and an expression belongs to the language when there is\n"
"a derivation of it. Every step you take is checked by Lean's kernel."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `X⁰ → X′`
#. §1: `X′ → XP`
#. §2: `YP X′ → XP`
#. §3: `X⁰ YP → X′`
#. §4: `X′ YP → X′`
#. §5: `Move α: YP X′ → XP`
#. §6: `rule \"X0 -> X'\" cat`
#. §7: `cat_N'`
#. §8: `cat_NP`
#: Game.Levels.Demo.L03_XBarGB
msgid "Same language, a different grammar.\n"
"\n"
"Categories are now head and bar level: N⁰, N′, NP, and so on. A category can also carry a\n"
"phrase that waits inside it to move: VP/DP is a VP with a waiting DP.\n"
"\n"
"The rules are the X-bar schema, five rules with variables X and Y over head categories,\n"
"plus Move α:\n"
"\n"
"- Rule 1: §0\n"
"- Rule 2: §1\n"
"- Rule 3: §2\n"
"- Rule 4: §3\n"
"- Rule 5: §4\n"
"- Rule 6: §5\n"
"\n"
"GB enters as conditions on their domains:\n"
"\n"
"- **c-selection:** C selects TP, T selects VP, V selects DP, D selects NP.\n"
"- **VP-internal subject:** the subject is merged in Spec-VP (Rule 3). V assigns it no Case,\n"
"  so it leaves a trace there and waits: the result is VP/DP.\n"
"- **Case:** V assigns accusative to its complement; finite T assigns nominative to its\n"
"  specifier. **Move α** puts the waiting DP into Spec-TP.\n"
"- **EPP:** T needs a specifier, so Rule 2 does not apply to T′; only Move α makes TP.\n"
"- **θ:** V assigns an external θ-role, so Rule 2 does not apply to V′ either: VP needs its\n"
"  subject.\n"
"- **Suffix lowering:** when X is T, Rule 4 attaches the suffix in T⁰ to the verb:\n"
"  see + -s = sees.\n"
"- **C** is empty. **Adjuncts:** none in this fragment, so Rule 5 never applies here.\n"
"\n"
"**Goal:** ⟨the cat sees the dog, CP⟩ ∈ L(xbarGB).\n"
"\n"
"Build from the bottom up, for example §6. Each result gets a name you can\n"
"use in the next step (§7, then §8, …). The lexicon is not used up."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `?`
#. §1: `copy(…)`
#. §2: `select the n cat will v sleep`
#. §3: `merge P Q`
#. §4: `the`
#. §5: `cat`
#. §6: `DP`
#. §7: `vP`
#. §8: `TP`
#. §9: `{DP, vP}`
#: Game.Levels.Demo.L04_MP
msgid "Same language, a grammar from a later era. Here **an expression is a workspace**.\n"
"Following Chomsky (2020, *UCLA Lectures*):\n"
"\n"
"  MERGE(P, Q, WS) = [{P, Q}, …] = WS′\n"
"\n"
"MERGE replaces P and Q by the set {P, Q}. If P and Q are both members of WS, this is\n"
"External MERGE, and WS loses one member. If P is a term of Q, it is Internal MERGE,\n"
"and WS keeps its size. One operation, not two.\n"
"\n"
"In Bare Grammar terms: the lexicon has one expression, the empty workspace.\n"
"Select and MERGE are rules whose domain is a decidable condition rather than a list\n"
"of categories.\n"
"\n"
"MERGE neither labels nor linearizes. Labels come from a labeling algorithm (after Chomsky 2013):\n"
"\n"
"- {H, XP}: H labels.\n"
"- {n, √root}, {v, √root}: a root cannot label, so n or v does.\n"
"- {XP, YP}: labeled ⟨φ,φ⟩ if both heads carry φ; otherwise no label —\n"
"  unless one of the two is a lower copy, which the algorithm does not see.\n"
"\n"
"Unlabeled nodes show as §0, lower copies as §1.\n"
"\n"
"**Goal:** a workspace with one object, every node labeled. Externalization — word order,\n"
"which copy is pronounced — is checked automatically. You only do narrow syntax.\n"
"\n"
"**Commands:**\n"
"\n"
"- §2 draws lexical items into WS.\n"
"- §3, where P and Q are lexical items (§4, §5), phrases named by their label\n"
"  (§6, §7, §8), or a set of two such names (§9).\n"
"\n"
"*Simplifications:* two occurrences of the same object count as copies;\n"
"Agree is not modeled (T's φ counts as valued); no phases."
msgstr ""

