#: Game.Levels.Demo.L03_XBarGB
msgid "Fourteen rule applications, one of them Move α: the subject was merged in Spec-VP, where V\n"
"gives it no Case, and moved to Spec-TP, where finite T assigns nominative. It left a trace.\n"
"\n"
"Suffix lowering is not the only analysis of *sees*. In Chomsky (1957) the same effect is\n"
"Affix Hopping, a transformation. Emonds (1978) and Pollock (1989) have the finite verb raise\n"
"to T in French, while in English only auxiliaries raise. In Chomsky (1993) the verb comes from\n"
"the lexicon already inflected and checks its features against T, so nothing lowers. Bobaljik\n"
"(1995) treats English tense and the verb as joining under adjacency at PF, and in Distributed\n"
"Morphology lowering is an operation after syntax (Halle & Marantz 1993; Embick & Noyer 2001).\n"
"Here lowering is a property of one rule: a different analysis changes that rule, not the\n"
"language."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `merge P Q`
#. §1: `the`
#. §2: `n`
#. §3: `cat`
#. §4: `will`
#. §5: `v`
#. §6: `sleep`
#. §7: `DP`
#. §8: `nP`
#. §9: `vP`
#. §10: `TP`
#. §11: `{DP, vP}`
#: Game.Metadata
msgid "§0 performs MERGE(P, Q, WS): it replaces P and Q by {P, Q}.\n"
"\n"
"P and Q can be written as\n"
"* a lexical item: §1, §2, §3, §4, §5, §6;\n"
"* a phrase named by its label: §7, §8, §9, §10;\n"
"* a set of two such names: §11.\n"
"\n"
"External MERGE: P and Q are both members of WS. Internal MERGE: one is a term\n"
"of the other. Anything else is rejected."
msgstr ""

